  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R14", "R15", "U1"],
      "pins": ["U1.8"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "/0V8_BK"],
      "observed": "The TPS62933 feedback divider in the POWER TRAIN block is R14 = 5.13 kΩ ('5k13') from the +5V node (U1 SW → L1 → +5V) down to the FB pin (/0V8_BK), with R15 = 10 kΩ from FB to GND.",
      "expected": "U1 (TPS62933, 0.8 V FB reference) must boost 0.8 V to a 5 V output, which requires R_top ≈ 52.5 kΩ (e.g. 52.3 kΩ, the E96 value used elsewhere on this sheet such as R20/R21 = 16k9) against R15 = 10 kΩ. A 5.13 kΩ top resistor programs Vout = 0.8 V × (1 + 5.13/10) ≈ 1.2 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U1 — TPS62933 (TI) | 24 V → 5 V synchronous buck regulator, 3 A"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "POWER TRAIN sheet: R14 marked '5k13', R15 marked '10k', L1 10µ from SW to +5V, FB net labelled /0V8_BK"}
      ],
      "impact": "The main +5V rail would regulate to roughly 1.2 V instead of 5 V. The ESP32-C6 DevKit's 5V0 input and the U2 AZ1117-3.3 LDO input would both be starved, so the board would not boot and the 3.3 V logic/isolator/TMC2209 VCC_IO domain would never come up.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "One deviation was found: the TPS62933 buck feedback divider (R14 = 5.13 kΩ with R15 = 10 kΩ) cannot produce the specified 5 V rail (it sets about 1.2 V), which would prevent the board from powering up. All other spec-stated values (I2C pull-ups 2.2 kΩ, TMC2209 sense resistors 110 mΩ, gate resistors 100 Ω, float pulls 10 kΩ, fuse 7 A, TVS 28 V, bulk caps 1000 µF/220 µF/47 µF) match the drawing; the confidence is limited because the R14 marking is small and could be a misread of a 51–52 kΩ value."
}

